+
+## Pitfalls
+
+1. **Reviewing the wrong base branch** — Always verify `gh pr view N` shows the correct base branch. Reviewing against `main` when the PR targets `develop` produces misleading diffs. Fix: Check the PR's target branch in the metadata before reviewing.
+
+2. **Stale HEAD_SHA for inline comments** — If new commits are pushed after you fetch the PR, your cached `HEAD_SHA` becomes stale and the review API returns 404. Fix: Always fetch the latest HEAD_SHA immediately before posting the review.
+
+3. **Posting comments to the Issues endpoint instead of Reviews** — Inline comments require the `/pulls/N/comments` endpoint with `commit_id`, not the `/issues/N/comments` endpoint. The Issues endpoint only supports top-level comments. Fix: Use the correct endpoint for inline vs top-level comments.
+
+4. **Forgetting to check out the PR branch** — Reviewing only the diff via `gh pr diff` misses context from surrounding code. For any non-trivial review, always check out the PR branch locally with `git fetch origin pull/N/head:pr-N && git checkout pr-N`.
+
+5. **Secret leakage in review comments** — If the diff contains accidentally committed secrets, your review comments will echo them back. Fix: Flag secrets as Critical issues without quoting the full secret value. Recommend immediate rotation.
+
+6. **Large PR overwhelm** — For PRs with 20+ changed files, reviewing everything in one pass produces shallow feedback. Fix: Focus on the highest-risk files first (auth, crypto, database, API routes) and explicitly note which files were not deeply reviewed.
+
+7. **Not cleaning up PR branches** — After reviewing, local `pr-N` branches accumulate. Fix: Always run `git checkout main && git branch -D pr-N` after posting the review.
